+Per the user's explicit instruction, asked before changing anything (AskUserQuestion: apply everywhere it applies / just the Stays tab / narrower scope). User chose "everywhere it applies." Applied, following round 16's established convention (the pack's own field labels verbatim, full text, no compression, no addresses/hours/prices/research-confidence/source lists):
+
+- **`kita-shinjuku`** rebuilt as a full 6-field About panel (Why it matters / A little context / Look for / How to experience it / Etiquette / sensitivity / One memorable detail), replacing the 2-row version.
+- **`shinjuku-shop`** given its first-ever About panel, same 6 fields, from the pack's Shinjuku entry.
+- **`late-food`** rebuilt as a proper 2-group accordion (Okubo / Shin-Okubo, 6 fields each) using `aboutItemsHTML()`'s existing grouped shape — the same mechanism Shibuya/Harajuku/Omotesando already use — instead of one flat, guessed group. The blurb was also updated to mention both areas ("Okubo and Shin-Okubo," was "Shin-Okubo" only) to match.
+- **Stay 2 (Komanoyu)** rebuilt as a 2-group accordion: "Komanoyu" (the ryokan itself — Priority A's richer 7-field entry, including the 1897 founding, the hinoki cypress bath, onsen etiquette) and "Kiso-Fukushima" (the town — Priority B's checkpoint-history entry).
+- **Stay 3 (Kabin Gion)** rebuilt as a 3-group accordion: "Gion," "Hanamikoji," and "Yasaka Shrine," all from Priority A, each with 6 fields including real history (Yasaka Shrine's 9th-century roots, the Gion Matsuri's 869 plague origin) and etiquette specifics.
+- Stay 4 (ARIA Takanawa) and the Kabin Gion/Isshuku Noma hotel addresses were left as they were — neither research pack covers hotels as subjects (they're place/area research), so there was nothing to upgrade there.
+
+**Real bug found and fixed along the way: mixing a flat Hotel row into a grouped `about` array broke `aboutItemsHTML()`'s shape detection.** The first version of `stayAboutHTML()` built `['Hotel', hotelValue].concat(areaRows)` and passed the combined array to `aboutItemsHTML()` in one call. That function decides flat-vs-grouped shape by checking ONLY the first entry (`!Array.isArray(rows[0])`) — fine when `areaRows` was itself flat (Stay 1), but once Stays 2 and 3 gained *grouped* `about` content (several named sub-places) in this same follow-up, the leading flat Hotel row made the whole array misdetect as flat, and the grouped entries after it would have been run through `aboutRowHTML()` (which reads `row[0]`/`row[1]` off what are actually `{group,tag,rows}` objects) and rendered incorrectly. Fixed by never concatenating the two: `stayAboutHTML()` now renders the Hotel row directly via `aboutRowHTML()` and passes `areaRows` to its own separate `aboutItemsHTML()` call, so each gets the correct code path regardless of the other's shape — both just render as plain sibling DOM nodes inside the same `.sheet-body`, so nothing looks different, but the flat-vs-grouped detection is no longer corrupted.
+
+**Second real bug found and fixed along the way: `#tabbar` had the exact same "stays hidden after visiting a day screen" bug round 17 already fixed once for `#refreshBtn`.** `closeDayFlip()`'s `finishClose()` deliberately returns to the home screen without calling `render()` (round 1's shimmer-avoidance fix) and had already been patched, in round 17, to manually un-hide `#refreshBtn` there since `render()` is the only other place that does. When `#tabbar` was added earlier this round, it wasn't given the same treatment — so opening any day and closing it back out left the segmented control hidden for the rest of the session, identical symptom and root cause to round 17's bug, just on a different element. Fixed the same way: `finishClose()` now also explicitly sets `tabbar.hidden = false` and resets its `.on` classes (Explorer on, Stays off, since `state.screen` is always `'home'` by the time this runs).
+
